{"tool_name":"gcta","contents":["biocontainers","bioschemas","biotools","bioconda","oeb"],"fetched_metadata":{"bioconda":{"name":"gcta","version":"1.94.1","home":"https://yanglab.westlake.edu.cn/software/gcta","license":"GPL-3.0-only","summary":"GCTA (Genome-wide Complex Trait Analysis) estimates the proportion of phenotypic variance explained by all genome-wide SNPs for complex traits.","identifiers":["biotools:gcta","doi:10.1016/j.ajhg.2010.11.011"],"build":{"number":0},"source_url":"https://yanglab.westlake.edu.cn/software/gcta/bin/gcta-1.94.1-linux-kernel-3-x86_64.zip","test":{"commands":["gcta | grep \"Analysis started\""]}},"biocontainers":{"name":"gcta","identifiers":["biotools:gcta","doi:10.1016/j.ajhg.2010.11.011"],"license":"MIT","summary":"A tool for genome-wide complex trait analysis","home_url":"http://cnsgenomics.com/software/gcta/index.html","keywords":["Chromosomes, Human, X","Computer Simulation","Dosage Compensation, Genetic","Female","Genetic Linkage","Genome-Wide Association Study","Humans","Male","Models, Genetic","Polymorphism, Single Nucleotide","Quantitative Trait Loci","GWAS study","Genomics","Genetics","Genetic variation","Transcriptome"],"manually_check":true,"total_pulls":0},"biotools":{"id":"gcta","home":"http://cnsgenomics.com/software/gcta/","license":"MIT","summary":"Genome-wide Complex Trait Analysis. Estimate the proportion of phenotypic variance explained by genome- or chromosome-wide SNPs for complex traits (the GREML method), and has subsequently extended for many other analyses to better understand the genetic architecture of complex traits.","addition_date":"2017-08-20T15:49:13Z","last_update_date":"2019-09-04T08:38:05Z","tool_type":["Command-line tool"],"version":["1.91.7beta"],"collections":["Animal and Crop Genomics"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:gcta","credit":[{"email":"jian.yang@uq.edu.au","name":"Jian Yang","orcidid":"http://orcid.org/0000-0003-2001-2474","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"]}],"documentation":[{"type":["General"],"url":"http://cnsgenomics.com/software/gcta/tutorial.html"},{"type":["User manual"],"url":"http://gcta.freeforums.net/board/2/gcta-user-manual"}],"edit_permission":{"authors":["animalandcropgenomics"],"type":"group"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Genetic variation analysis","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3197"}]}],"name":"GCTA","operating_system":["Linux"],"owner":"admin","publication":[{"doi":"10.1016/j.ajhg.2010.11.011","metadata":{"abstract":"For most human complex diseases and traits, SNPs identified by genome-wide association studies (GWAS) explain only a small fraction of the heritability. Here we report a user-friendly software tool called genome-wide complex trait analysis (GCTA), which was developed based on a method we recently developed to address the \"missing heritability\" problem. GCTA estimates the variance explained by all the SNPs on a chromosome or on the whole genome for a complex trait rather than testing the association of any particular SNP to the trait. We introduce GCTA's five main functions: data management, estimation of the genetic relationships from SNPs, mixed linear model analysis of variance explained by the SNPs, estimation of the linkage disequilibrium structure, and GWAS simulation. We focus on the function of estimating the variance explained by all the SNPs on the X chromosome and testing the hypotheses of dosage compensation. The GCTA software is a versatile tool to estimate and partition complex trait variation with large GWAS data sets. \u00a9 2011 The American Society of Human Genetics.","authors":[{"name":"Goddard M.E."},{"name":"Lee S.H."},{"name":"Visscher P.M."},{"name":"Yang J."}],"citationCount":2962,"date":"2011-01-07T00:00:00Z","journal":"American Journal of Human Genetics","title":"GCTA: A tool for genome-wide complex trait analysis"},"pmcid":"PMC3014363","pmid":"21167468","type":["Primary"]}],"topic":[{"term":"GWAS study","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3517"}],"validated"